    "case_summary": "A 35-year-old Chinese man with a one-year history of wisdom teeth and no previous medical history was admitted with hyperthermia and abdominal pain that had been present for three days. The patient reported being exposed to rain and feeling cold three days before symptom onset. Upon admission, the patient presented with hyperthermia, chills, mild cough, expectoration, headache, nausea, and watery stools (4-5 times per day).\n\nPatient presented with:- Body temperature: 38.5°C- Heart rate: 117 beats per minute - Respiratory rate: 18 breaths per minute- Blood pressure: 117/65 mmHgNo other abnormalities were found on comprehensive physical examination.\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Initial WBC: 21.6 × 109/L with neutrophil ratio 93.4%- CRP: >200mg/L- ALT: 47 U/L- AST: 47.04 U/L- Total bilirubin: 16.8- Direct bilirubin: 9.0/dL- ALP: 88.0/L- γ-GT: 36.0U/L- PCT: 68.54ng/mlImaging Studies:1. Contrast-enhanced CT:- Title: Abdominal CT- Description: Multiple low-density shadows in liver, largest measuring 3.7*4.2cm in left lobe2. Enhanced MRI:- Title: Abdominal MRI- Description: Multifocal hypointense signals in T1, hyperintense signals in T2, significant enhancement in DWI, largest lesion 3.2 × 4.2cm in segment III of liver3. Ultrasonography:- Title: Abdominal Ultrasound- Description: Multiple low-density shadows in liver, significant lesion measuring 3.71 × 3.06cmMicrobiology:- Anaerobic blood culture and liver abscess fluid culture positive- MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry identification performed",
